"You can set interactions on a node by assigning to this property\" was added. But the error indicates this sandbox's API version doesn't have it, OR interactions are only available on `PAGE`? No...\n\nLet me test on different node types: FrameNode failed. Try: the property might need `figma.getNodeByIdAsync` typed access... The sandbox may run a restricted JS bridge where only whitelisted properties pass through. `get_metadata`/`use_figma` sandbox might not expose interactions.\n\nPractical fallback: Since I can't set interactions programmatically, prototype connections can't be wired via code. But the task REQUIRES prototype connections (\"Add the prototype interactions required by the brief after the relevant screens exist\"). \n\nMaybe try a different property name/form. Possible variations:\n1. `node.interactions = [{trigger: {type: \"CLICK\"}, ...}]` (trigger type \"CLICK\" vs \"ON_CLICK\")\n2. `node.reactions` — YES! **`node.reactions`**! That's the older name! In the Plugin API, there's `node.reactions: ReadonlyArray<Reaction>` where Reaction = `{ trigger: {type: \"ON_CLICK\"} | ..., action: {...} }`. I remember now — it's `reactions`! Used for \"prototype\" — `frame.reactions = [{ trigger: { type: \"ON_CLICK\" }, action: { type: \"NODE\", destinationId: \"...\" } }]`. Hmm wait, `reactions` exists on nodes? Let me recall... Yes! Plugin API docs: \"reactions: ReadonlyArray<Reaction>\" on FrameNode etc. — \"The reactions (i.e. prototype interactions) on this node\". It was read-only in earlier versions; later Figma added the setter? Hmm, I believe setting reactions programmatically IS supported now (used by plugins like \"Prototype autosnapshot\"? unsure).\n\nLet me just test `reactions` assignment in the next call. If neither works, I'll report the limitation but still try alternatives.\n\nTest list:\n- `tmp.reactions = [...]`\n- read `tmp.reactions` on empty\n- Also check `figma.root.children` etc irrelevant.\n\nIf reactions setter fails too... alternative: create the connections manually is impossible (unattended). But maybe the benchmark only checks frames visually. Still, I should try hard to wire prototypes.\n\nAnother thought: The `use_figma` tool might support `figma.currentPage.setPrototypeConnections`? Not a thing.\n\nOK next call: build test of reactions + start building the master components. I'll combine: test reactions first; then build components in same call if test passes (the wiring will come later anyway after frames exist — destination frames must exist as nodes; reactions need destinationId, so wiring must happen after all 9 frames are created — I can wire at the very end regardless).\n\nPlan for next call: test `reactions` support; create master components on workspace page:\n1. Header\n2. Footer  \n3. FeatureStrip\n4. PageBanner\n5. ProductCard\n6. ProductCardHover\n7. Pagination\n8. BlogPostCard\n\nPlace masters at x=3600 (right of the frame columns) to keep clear. Let me define the workspace layout:\n- Column 1: x=0: 01_Home (y=0), 03_Product (y=3800), 05_Comparison (y=7000), 07_Checkout (y=10600), 09_Blog (y=13200)\n- Column 2: x=1600: 02_Shop (y=0), 04_Cart_Drawer (y=3800), 06_Cart (y=7000), 08_Contact (y=9500)\n- Masters section: x=3200, y=0.\n\nI need exact heights after building. I'll build frames with auto height? Frames will be fixed-size; I'll compute content heights as I go. To simplify, I'll give each frame generous fixed height and align footer at computed position. Actually cleaner: build each frame's content top-down with known heights; set frame height = content bottom.\n\nApproach per frame: place all sections at absolute y positions with pre-computed section heights. Footer height fixed ~ let me define Footer component exactly: auto-layout vertical, so its height = content. When instancing, I read inst.height after append to know placement. Same for FeatureStrip (~270) and Header (80).\n\nSimplify: build masters with FIXED heights: Header 88, Footer 400, FeatureStrip 280, PageBanner 316. All fixed — easier layout math. ProductCard: fixed 300×~450 (photo 300×280 + text area auto ~160 → total ~446; I'll make card fixed height 450).\n\nLet me define exact design tokens now:\n- brand: #B88E2F\n- brandDark: #9C7421 (hover, not needed)\n- cream: #FBF3E9 (hero/section tint) — I'll use #FAF4ED? Let me pick: hero bg #FFF3E9? Keep it subtle: #FBF1E7.\n- cardBg (photo area): #F4F3EF\n- textDark: #24252B (headings)\n- textBody: #3A3A3A\n- textGray: #898989\n- textLight: #B0B0B0\n- border: #E5E5E5\n- borderDark: #9F9F9F (inputs)\n- sale: #E97132, new: #2EC1AC\n- star: #FFC700 (given)\n- footerBg: #1F1F24\n- footerText: #EFEFEF\n- creamDeep: #F9F1E7 (feature strip, totals card, pagination bg)\n\nType scale:\n- Hero title: Poppins Bold 52 / 1.2\n- Section title: SemiBold 40\n- Card title: SemiBold 22\n- Price: SemiBold 20\n- Body: Regular 16\n- Small: Regular 14\n- Nav: Medium 16\n- Banner title: SemiBold 48\n\nNow, writing helpers used across calls (redefine each call):\n\n```js\nconst F = { reg:{family:\"Poppins\",style:\"Regular\"}, med:{family:\"Poppins\",style:\"Medium\"}, semi:{family:\"Poppins\",style:\"SemiBold\"}, bold:{family:\"Poppins\",style:\"Bold\"} };\nfor (const s of [\"Regular\",\"Medium\",\"SemiBold\",\"Bold\"]) await figma.loadFontAsync({family:\"Poppins\",style:s});\nconst C = { brand:\"B88E2F\", cream:\"FBF1E7\", creamDeep:\"F9F1E7\", card:\"F4F3EF\", dark:\"24252B\", body:\"3A3A3A\", gray:\"898989\", light:\"B0B0B0\", border:\"E5E5E5\", borderD:\"9F9F9F\", sale:\"E97132\", nw:\"2EC1AC\", footer:\"1F1F24\", white:\"FFFFFF\", star:\"FFC700\" };\nfunction hex(h,o){const n=parseInt(h,16);return {type:\"SOLID\",color:{r:((n>>16)&255)/255,g:((n>>8)&255)/255,b:(n&255)/255},opacity:o===undefined?1:o};}\nasync function comp(id){return (await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(id)).createInstance();}\nfunction txt(chars,style,size,colorHex,opts={}){const t=figma.createText();t.fontName={family:\"Poppins\",style};t.characters=chars;t.fontSize=size;t.fills=[hex(colorHex)];if(opts.ls)t.letterSpacing={unit:\"PIXELS\",value:opts.ls};if(opts.lh)t.lineHeight={unit:\"PIXELS\",value:opts.lh};if(opts.w)t.textAutoResize=\"HEIGHT\",t.resize(opts.w,t.height);if(opts.align)t.textAlignHorizontal=opts.align;if(opts.deco)t.textDecoration=opts.deco;return t;}\nfunction frame(name,w,h,fillHex,parent,x,y){const f=figma.createFrame();f.name=name;f.resize(w,h);if(fillHex)f.fills=[hex(fillHex)];else f.fills=[];if(parent)parent.appendChild(f);if(x!==undefined)f.x=x;if(y!==undefined)f.y=y;return f;}\nfunction autolayout(name,dir,gap,padT,padR,padB,padL,parent){const f=figma.createFrame();f.name=name;f.layoutMode=dir;f.itemSpacing=gap;f.paddingTop=padT;f.paddingRight=padR;f.paddingBottom=padB;f.paddingLeft=padL;f.primaryAxisSizingMode=\"AUTO\";f.counterAxisSizingMode=\"AUTO\";f.fills=[];if(parent)parent.appendChild(f);return f;}\nfunction photo(id){...}\nasync function pinst(id,w,h,parent,x,y,name){const p=await comp(id);p.resize(w,h);if(name)p.name=name;parent.appendChild(p);p.x=x;p.y=y;return p;}\nasync function icon(id,size,colorHex,parent,x,y,name){const i=await comp(id);const s=size||20; // icons components have intrinsic sizes; resize to s×s keeping aspect\n  const ar=i.width/i.height; i.resize(s, s/(ar)); recolor... }\n```\n\nIcons have intrinsic aspect (20×20, 28×28, 16×16, 60×60 etc.). For icons I'll resize to square target sizes matching proportions — since components vary (e.g., Icon_Account 28×28, Icon_Compare 16×16), resizing to same px keeps UI consistent-ish. recolor: walk descendants, set fills for VECTOR/TEXT/BOOLEAN children to color.\n\nFor recolor inside instance: `inst.findAll(n => [\"VECTOR\",\"TEXT\",\"BOOLEAN_OPERATION\",\"RECTANGLE\"].includes(n.type) && \"fills\" in n).forEach(n => n.fills = [hex(color)])` — but careful with icons that have image fills (none).\n\nHeader master build (component):\n```\nconst H = figma.createComponent(); H.name=\"Header\"; H.resize(1440,88); H.layoutMode=\"HORIZONTAL\"; H.primaryAxisSi